## Why This Is Asked

This question tests non-blocking IPC design under kernel constraints, focusing on memory ordering, CAS-based synchronization, wraparound handling, and backpressure mechanisms.

## Key Concepts

- **Non-blocking SPSC ring design**: Single-producer, single-consumer architecture eliminates locking overhead
- **Atomic operations and memory barriers**: Ensure proper ordering of loads and stores without locks
- **Cacheline padding**: Align head/tail indices to separate cache lines, preventing false sharing between CPU cores
- **Wraparound logic**: Use modulo arithmetic or bit-masking (when N is a power of 2) to handle circular buffer indexing
- **Overflow signaling**: Track the distance between head and tail to detect full/empty conditions; use separate flags or sequence numbers for accurate state detection
- **Burst workload testing**: Verify no data loss, no misordering, and no stalls under high-throughput scenarios

## Memory Layout

```c
typedef struct { int data; uint64_t seq; } item_t;

typedef struct {
    _Atomic uint64_t head L1_CACHE_LINE_ALIGNED;
    _Atomic uint64_t tail L1_CACHE_LINE_ALIGNED;
    char pad1[L1_CACHE_LINE_SIZE - sizeof(_Atomic uint64_t)];
    item_t buf[N];
    char pad2[L1_CACHE_LINE_SIZE];  // Tail padding to prevent write-through
} ring_buffer_t;
```

## Enqueue (Producer)

```c
bool enqueue(ring_buffer_t* rb, item_t* item) {
    uint64_t head = atomic_load_explicit(&rb->head, memory_order_acquire);
    uint64_t next_head = (head + 1) & (N - 1);  // Power-of-2 wraparound

    if (next_head == atomic_load_explicit(&rb->tail, memory_order_acquire)) {
        return false;  // Buffer full (one slot reserved)
    }

    rb->buf[head] = *item;
    atomic_store_explicit(&rb->head, next_head, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}
```

## Dequeue (Consumer)

```c
bool dequeue(ring_buffer_t* rb, item_t* item) {
    uint64_t tail = atomic_load_explicit(&rb->tail, memory_order_acquire);
    uint64_t head = atomic_load_explicit(&rb->head, memory_order_acquire);

    if (tail == head) {
        return false;  // Buffer empty
    }

    *item = rb->buf[tail];
    uint64_t next_tail = (tail + 1) & (N - 1);
    atomic_store_explicit(&rb->tail, next_tail, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}
```

## Test Plan

| Test Case | Method | Pass Criteria |
|-----------|--------|---------------|
| **Data loss detection** | Inject known sequence numbers, compare enqueued vs. dequeued count | Zero missing sequence numbers |
| **Misordering detection** | Tag each item with monotonic sequence ID, verify strictly increasing order on dequeue | Sequence IDs monotonically increase |
| **Burst traffic** | Enqueue N items rapidly, then dequeue all; repeat with varying N | All items recovered, correct order |
| **Concurrent stress** | Run producer/consumer threads on separate cores, measure throughput | No stalls, no corruption |
| **Wraparound stress** | Fill buffer, drain, repeat N > capacity times | No indexing errors after multiple wraparounds |
| **Overflow handling** | Attempt enqueue when full, verify rejection | Returns false, no corruption |
| **Empty buffer** | Attempt dequeue when empty, verify rejection | Returns false, no corruption |

## Why This Question Is Asked

Assesses understanding of scalable synchronization across cores and NUMA nodes, combining MVCC, CAS-based publish, and cross-node invalidation. Tests the ability to reason about contention, migration costs, and correctness guarantees in a realistic service-oriented workload.

## Key Concepts

- **MVCC with per-object version counters** enabling concurrent readers without blocking
- **CAS-based publish** and per-object coarse-grained locking for safe writes
- **NUMA-aware traffic routing** and lazy migration strategies to minimize cross-node latency
- **Batched invalidations** to reduce cross-core and cross-node traffic overhead

## Code Example

```c
// Minimal MVCC implementation for acquire/read/commit paths

typedef struct {
    uint64_t version;
    void*    data;
} Object;

// Acquire: snapshot version for consistent read
uint64_t acquire(Object* obj) {
    return obj->version;
}

// Read: fetch data, revalidate version
void* read(Object* obj, uint64_t snap) {
    void* val = obj->data;
    if (__sync_val_compare_and_swap(&obj->version, snap, snap) != snap) {
        return read(obj, acquire(obj)); // retry on concurrent write
    }
    return val;
}

// Commit: atomic swap with incremented version
int commit(Object* obj, void* new_data) {
    Object* new_obj = allocate_staging();
    new_obj->data = new_data;
    new_obj->version = obj->version + 1;
    return __sync_bool_compare_and_swap(&obj, obj, new_obj);
}
```

Under high contention, the retry loop in `read()` converges quickly as CAS failures indicate write-in-flight, and the coarse-grained lock per shard limits serialization to critical sections only, preserving throughput across cores.

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Races in io_uring multi-producer submission and memory ordering are subtle in real systems; candidates demonstrate understanding of ring buffers, memory barriers, and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- io_uring submission and completion rings (SQ/CQ)
- Multi-producer correctness in shared ring buffers
